an in my experience  when I talk to my cell phone provider or  even a credit card that I want to cancel  rarely is whoever you initially talk to  actually empower to cancel that account  and in my experience and tell me if this  is good or bad advice when I'm calling  specifically because of a problem with  my bill or  want to negotiate a better rate I just  find I go directly to retentions whether  that's by saying to the phone tree that  I want to cancel my account or just  finding the retentions number is this a  good strategy in other words are  consumers going to save themselves some  time by going directly to a retentions  agent well it is true that there are  deals that are offered by retention that  aren't offered by any other departments  it isn't necessarily true that you'll  get to retention if you say you're going  to disconnect gotcha okay so we'll go  through the process with with Time  Warner so when you got a call what at  what point in the process were those  customers had they already spoken to a  regular customer service agent and had  indicated they want to cancel almost  always these people have been on the  line first sometimes upwards of two  hours okay so you get someone your phone  rings you take the call maybe they've  been on the phone for two hours are you  able to see how long total the person  has been on the phone you know sometimes  you get transferred around five six  seven times do you know what the  transfer history and total call length  has been absolutely not a lot of the  time another agent will just put them on  hold and say they'll be right back and  they'll be transferred to you  so when you greet them they're like what  I was just talking to someone else right  okay so let's talk a little bit about  the training process when you were  brought in what were you told was  ultimately the goal of what your job was  there to do what was there to accomplish  were you there to help people sort out  their bills were you there simply to  prevent people from canceling at all  costs how was your position described  the position is described as you're put  in place to help the customer realize  the value of their product so they won't  cancel gotcha and what tools and  techniques were the ones that you were  told you should be using to prevent  people from canceling de-escalation  mainly these people are usually pretty  upset when they get through to us  it's de-escalation restating the value  offering more services for a shorter  amount of time for a lower rate okay and  was there ever really a specific  situation where you were told listen if  someone calls  with this scenario you don't have to try  to upsell them or retain them if someone  calls because they're I don't know  they're the person who had the account  died for example redo you just say ok  we'll disconnect for you if they're  actually dead  they have to take a death certificate in  to the local payment center so we can't  actually do that over the line if  they're sick or something though you can  absolutely upsell and was that what you  were told to do so if someone calls in  and they say you know where my husband's  just really ill and either for financial  or other reasons we just don't really  want or need this anymore you were still  told in that case offer them more  expensive stuff right and how does that  work tell us a little bit about that  you'd say you first empathize say I'm I  can understand all that would be hard  listen is he going to be laid up for a  while what kind of things does he like  to watch on TV maybe we can add this NBA  Sports Pass or something like that and  we're there what would you say was was  the well let's let's I'll ask him two  ways  what was your success rate in terms of  the calls you got in getting people to  ultimately not cancel and was there an  expected or target success rate that was  established in other words what were the  metrics that were put in place the  metrics have put in place for how much  money you're actually saving a lot of  the time you can actually get a pay  raise by saving the company money by  preventing these people from  disconnecting but if you were to slip  below that rating you'd actually just go  back to the your initial flat pay rate  my actual rate of save I'm not sure what  I'd say probably it was pretty high  maybe like 70% so in other words that  means that of every 10 calls you could  save 70% or 7 of those people you could  prevent them from canceling right  because a lot of people they do call in  because they're frustrated about prices  if you can work with them a lot of the  time they do stay with the company just  if you're willing to work with them what  was the turnover rate in other words the  people that worked in this retention  department was it  a position in which people would stay  for a while where people just constantly  coming in and out and why were they  leaving constantly in and out everyone  was leaving just because of the actual  environment it's really hard to for  people to stay positive in a place where  everyone is so negative that you're  talking to on the phone yeah I mean it  would it would seem to me that it would  just become not only an incredibly  repetitive situation but also your your  like you said you're dealing with people  who are angry all the time I imagine  that that gets to you it absolutely did  yeah so I've been having as I've talked  about on the program an issue with Time  Warner here in New York City where I  think it's been 17 phone calls just  trying to get the price and package that  I was initially promised 13 months ago  when I moved to New York City it's  almost become kind of like a game where  I just wonder what are they going to  tell me next and every time I'll  reference the story up until that point  to kind of get us both on you know on  kind of equal footing is there any  accountability like does anyone care  what anyone else is said to me no no I'm  sorry the promises that are made what we  were supposed to never throw anyone  under the bus never say no they lied to  you what we're supposed to say is let me  look into that and I don't think that's  something we can actually do but let me  see what I can't do for you I've heard  that many many times so but and  ultimately often times I'll be told okay  listen David you know we we've got this  sorted out I apologize for the trouble  you've had what we set up is that your  next bill will be this amount and that's  what it will be for the next nine months  and then I get my next bill and it's $30  more in those cases was the last agent I  spoke to were they just straight-up  lying to me to get me off of the phone  did they believe that they had offered  that they had put in place the price I  was given and through some other  mechanism it didn't happen or what  exactly is going on behind the scenes  there it's funny you should ask that way  because it could be that they didn't do  it because there's no accountability it  could be that they try to do it but the  software wouldn't let it go through or  it could be that they tried to save you  money but the company actually wouldn't  approve the credit so sometimes somebody  might actually put in  say we are gonna give this customer this  credit and then at some point after we  get off the phone but before my next  bill bill comes in  someone who's higher up than the phone  agent said oh no no no we're not gonna  be doing this exactly and then they're  under no obligation or at least decide  they're not even gonna communicate that  to the customer I would guess no and  they don't tell the agency there so we  have no idea that's that's just  incredible  hey last thing I want to ask you about  you talked a little bit about how  depending on how many of these accounts  that people want to cancel how many of  them you're saving that there's kind of  like a variable pay or bonus structure  so how does that work is that a  significant proportion of someone's base  salary in other words can your salary  really change drastically based on how  many people you're convincing not to  cancel absolutely not  no at most it's a $2 thing and that goes  by every two weeks depending on how well  you're doing it's nothing that you could  strive for if you know what I mean  understood okay and then how about as  far as what tends to happen to everybody  which is you get one price for six  months and then it goes up and then it  goes up and that entire kind of the  billing creep that takes place what's  the best way to prevent that or at least  to to counteract it it would be to keep  in touch with them one thing that is the  reason that that's sort of justified in  the company anyway is because you don't  actually have a contract like these  other companies so you can call in and  adjust rates it's not I mean it's  obviously not the best practice to keep  changing the rates I'm not trying to  defend that but you are able to  negotiate with them all right so it  seems that that is with it's almost like  you just need to set a calendar reminder  every three months you should be calling  up to negotiate I can't I can't say  whether or not that's true all right  we've been speaking with Jackie Keaton  former Time Warner Cable retentions  agent Jackie thanks for being with us  absolutely thanks for having me David

